// WebGLEngine/tools/ship/trainSubPilot-selfcheck.mjs -- UNNUMBERED (beside tools/ship/trainSubPilot.mjs)
//
// Run: node tools/ship/trainSubPilot-selfcheck.mjs      (~90 s)
//
// GRADES THE TOOL THAT TRAINS THE SUBMARINE'S PILOT ON A CIRCUIT, NOT THE PILOTS (tools/ship/trainDronePilot-selfcheck.mjs is the quadrotor's, and this is the same gate over the same recipe on the submarine's job). The tool's whole value is that it cannot flatter
// itself: several independent searches; a search is ELIGIBLE only if it ends no dive early on a pool of 500 starts that the autopilot completes, and no more dives early than the autopilot does; the champion is the best held-out of the eligible; the audit, the 500-dive
// safety audit and the reserve are read ONCE and only for the champion (and not at all when no search is eligible); and a file is written only when that champion beats the autopilot on the held-out, audit and safety-audit scores, ends no dive early that the
// autopilot completes, keeps its worst-5% tail, loses no kind of dive, and does not dive the reserve worse. So this gate holds: the argument parser refuses what it does not know (a typo must not silently train the default recipe), the tail arithmetic and the
// qualification rule on tables of made-up results (each clause is the only thing that fails in its row, for the audit AND for the safety audit), the scenario sets (the six sets of one verdict share no dive, and share none with a set an earlier champion was graded
// on), finish() driven with a STAND-IN POLICY whose dives the gate controls (so that "the better search is the dangerous one" is a fact of the setup and not a hope about a real search), the searches in child processes giving the bits the in-process ones give, the
// command with its side effects injected (it writes nothing without --write and nothing when the champion does not qualify), and the blob it writes, for the three circuits. brain/pilotSubPolicy-selfcheck.mjs and brain/pilotSubFish-selfcheck.mjs hold the networks.
//
// SECTION 9 is the shipped fish pilot (brain/pilotSubWeightsFishWip.json): it recomputes every number in the file's `by`, on the audits that were read for it.
//
// SABOTAGE LOG (70 mutants of tools/ship/trainSubPilot.mjs, each applied in a scratch tree together with a COPY of the command file tools/ship/trainPilotSub.mjs so that the gate's child processes run the mutated library and not the real
//   one, the gate run on the tree, the tree discarded; the number is how many rows went red; NONE LEFT AT 0 ON THE FIRST FULL RUN). They are the drone trainer's mutants written again for this module's text and for the circuit table and the audits:
//   S01 RECIPE iterations 800 3; S02 AUDIT_BASE 6000 (a spent range) 4; S03 KIND_SLACK 0.05 4; S04 RESERVE_SLACK 0.05 1; S05 the fly seed 6001 2; S06 the fish seed 6101 5; S07 the control seed 6201 1; S08 the fish has no default file 1; S09 the fish default file renamed 1; S10 the fish blob marked for the fly 4; S11 the fish is the control's network 1; S12 the fly is the fish's network 6; S13 CIRCUITS not frozen 1; S14 no circuit named is the fish 2; S15 an unknown circuit accepted by circuitOf 1; S16 an unknown option accepted 3; S17 an unknown circuit accepted by the parser 1; S18 the circuit's seed ignored 4; S19 the circuit's default file ignored 1; S20 an early end judged on "crashed" only 2; S21 tails counts no dives 2; S22 the new-early-end clause dropped 4; S23 the more-early-ends clause dropped 2; S24 held-out > loosened to >= 2; S25 the newly-ended clause dropped 3; S26 the early-ends clause dropped 3; S27 the mean > loosened to >= 2; S28 the tail clause loosened by 5 3; S29 the kind clause dropped 5; S30 the safety audit left out of the rule 7; S31 the kinds read from the audit for both 2; S32 the reserve score clause dropped 2; S33 the reserve early-end clause dropped 2; S34 the pool is the fresh safety audit 2; S35 the audit is the spent first-round audit 3; S36 the safety audit is the spent first-round one 4; S37 the reserve is the held-out set 2; S38 searchOne ignores --slice 4; S39 searchOne returns the unpruned weights 3; S40 searchOne's seed is not offset by k 3; S41 eligibility ignored 6; S42 the champion picked on the train score 2; S43 eligibility judged on the held-out set 5; S44 the audit read from the held-out set 2; S45 the safety audit read from the audit set 2; S46 the reserve read from the held-out set 3; S47 auditVs against the held-out of the autopilot 2; S48 safetyVs against itself 3; S49 the autopilot's audit read from the held-out 2; S50 the dump leaves the weights out 1; S51 the dump's circuit is always the fly 2; S52 the file leaves the known limits out 1; S53 the blob carries no circuit mark 3; S54 the blob's score is the audit's 1; S55 every blob is named for the fish 1; S56 the blob quotes the moved count as the kept one 1; S57 --write writes though the champion does not qualify 1; S58 --write with --slice allowed 1; S59 --write without a file allowed 1; S60 --one runs search K+1 3; S61 the children get no circuit 1; S62 the children get no slice 1; S63 the children's results in reverse order 1; S64 --dump not written 1; S65 the hash line says 0 1; S66 a refused write exits 0 1; S67 a bad argument exits 1 2; S68 the children run with one worker whatever --workers says 1; S69 the safety audit not in the log verdict line 1; S70 the eligibility count clause on the pool ignored 5.
//   (S02 AUDIT_BASE 6000 is a range an earlier champion spent; S35 and S36 put the first round's audit and safety audit in; S11 and S12 wire the fish to the control's network and the fly to the fish's; S10 marks the fish blob for the fly: each a failed row by name.)
//   THE FIRST RUN OF THE GATE ITSELF FOUND THREE THINGS IN THE GATE, NOT THE TOOL: the stand-in's 'bold' search held out no better than the safe one (its extra early ends were in the held-out dives too -- they are now only in seeds 1000 and over); the
//   child-process rows compared a search that ended at the autopilot's weights (two iterations on a seventh of the dives: moved 6, kept 0), which cannot tell search 0 from nothing -- four iterations keep two weights and are asserted to differ; and a
//   row's expectation (what the 'kind slack' reads) was stated for the wrong kind.
"use strict";
import fs from "node:fs";
